.TH SYNCTANG\-UNLOCKER 8 "" "synctang" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
synctang\-unlocker \- unlock a LUKS volume with the approval of a key holder
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B synctang\-unlocker
.I command
.RI [ flags ]
.PP
.B synctang\-unlocker enrol
.B \-\-device
.I device
.B \-\-pubkey
.I hex
.B \-\-existing\-passphrase\-file
.I file
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B synctang\-unlocker pair
.B \-\-device
.I device
.B \-\-existing\-passphrase\-file
.I file
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B synctang\-unlocker status
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B synctang\-unlocker devices
.br
.B synctang\-unlocker agent
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B synctang\-unlocker open
.RI [ flags ]
.I device
.RI [ name ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B synctang\-unlocker
is the machine side of synctang, an on-demand unlocker for LUKS2 volumes.
Instead of typing a passphrase at the console, a person approves the unlock
from a key holder, which is the laptop program
.BR synctang\-keyholder (1)
or the synctang phone app.
.PP
Each enrolled key holder has its own keyslot on the volume and an entry in a
LUKS2 token of type
.BR mr\-1 ,
written into the volume's header.
The secret that opens the keyslot is not kept on the machine in the clear: the
token holds it only in encrypted form, and it is recovered from the key holder's
answer, checked against the volume, and handed on.
Machine and key holder find each other through Syncthing's relay and discovery
servers, or on the local network, and each side checks the other's Device ID.
By default the public Syncthing servers are used; see
.BR synctang\-network.conf (5)
to use your own.
.PP
At boot the
.B agent
command runs inside the dracut initrd, beside the ordinary console prompt
for the passphrase; whichever is answered first wins.
The other commands are run by a person on the machine.
.SH COMMANDS
Every command accepts
.B \-h
and prints its flags and exits with status 2.
Flags may be written with one or two dashes.
.SS enrol
Adds a key holder's public key to the volume: a new keyslot, holding a fresh
random secret, and a new recipient in the volume's
.B mr\-1
token.
It needs the privileges
.BR cryptsetup (8)
needs on the device, normally root, and an existing passphrase of the volume.
It then prints this machine's transport identity, which the key holder needs
to find the machine.
.PP
There is one enrolled key holder per transport identity.
If the identity given with
.B \-\-recipient\-transport\-id
is already enrolled with the same key, nothing is added and the command prints
.BI "already enrolled at keyslot " N
and succeeds.
If it is enrolled with a different key, the command fails and says to use
.B \-\-replace ,
naming the keyslot and the old key.
.PP
With
.B \-\-remove
it revokes a key holder, destroying its keyslot and removing its entry from the
token, instead of adding one.
With
.B \-\-replace
it swaps a key holder's key for a new one, enrolling the new key before the
old one is revoked, so the volume can be opened throughout.
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fIpath\fR
The LUKS2 device or image to enrol on.
Required.
.TP
\fB\-\-pubkey\fR \fIhex\fR
The key holder's public key, hex-encoded, as printed by
.B synctang\-keyholder export\-pubkey .
Required, except with
.BR \-\-remove .
.TP
\fB\-\-existing\-passphrase\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
A file holding an existing valid passphrase of the volume, or
.B \-
for standard input.
One trailing newline is ignored.
Required.
Do not keep the passphrase in a file on disk; see
.BR EXAMPLES .
A process substitution, as in the examples, does not survive
.BR sudo (8),
which closes every file descriptor but the first three before it runs the
command: run the examples from a root shell, or send the passphrase on standard
input with
.B \-\-existing\-passphrase\-file \-
(not for
.BR pair ,
whose confirmation question also reads standard input).
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fIname\fR
This machine's name, shown to the key holder before it approves.
It is a label chosen by the machine and is not authenticated.
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\-id\fR \fIid\fR
This machine's transport identity, recorded in the token and shown to the key
holder.
.TP
\fB\-\-recipient\-transport\-id\fR \fIid\fR
The key holder's own transport identity, as printed by
.BR "synctang\-keyholder export\-pubkey" ,
recorded with its entry so that the agent answers only that key holder.
With
.B \-\-replace
it defaults to the identity being replaced.
.TP
\fB\-\-machine\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This machine's persistent transport identity.
Default
.BR /etc/synctang/machine.pem .
It is created if it does not exist.
When enrolling a disk image that belongs to another machine, name the identity
inside that image here, or the key holder is told a Device ID that no machine
announces.
.TP
\fB\-\-remove\fR \fIid\fR
Revoke the enrolled key holder with this transport identity.
Needs
.B \-\-device
and
.BR \-\-existing\-passphrase\-file .
.TP
\fB\-\-replace\fR \fIid\fR
Rotate the enrolled key holder with this transport identity to the key given
with
.BR \-\-pubkey .
.TP
\fB\-\-keyslot\fR \fIn\fR
With
.B \-\-remove
or
.BR \-\-replace :
the keyslot of the entry to act on.
Needed only when several enrolled entries share the transport identity, a state
that
.B status
warns about.
Without it, several matches are refused and listed, rather than the first being
chosen.
.SS pair
Shows a QR code and a one-time pairing code on the console, and enrols the key
holder that answers it.
The phone app scans the code; its text form is a
.B synctang://pair
link, printed under the code, for a camera that cannot scan.
Before anything is written to the volume, the person at the machine is shown
the Device ID and public key of the key holder that answered, and is asked to
confirm.
If that Device ID is already enrolled with the same key, nothing is asked and
nothing is added: the command says so and succeeds.
If it is enrolled with a different key, as when a phone's app was updated and
made a new key but kept its Device ID, the question names both keys and the old
keyslot, and asks whether to replace the existing entry with the new one.
On yes the new key is enrolled first and the old one revoked after it, so the
volume can be opened throughout; on no, or with no answer, nothing is changed.
.B \-\-yes
answers yes to either question.
The pairing code is good once, for the length of the window.
A wrong code or a refusal is reported and the command carries on waiting.
.PP
The passphrase is checked against the volume first, so a typo is found before
anyone has scanned anything.
The command needs the privileges
.BR cryptsetup (8)
needs on the device, normally root.
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fIpath\fR
The LUKS2 device or image to enrol the key holder on.
Required.
.TP
\fB\-\-existing\-passphrase\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
A file holding an existing valid passphrase of the volume, or
.B \-
for standard input.
Required.
If standard input is used, the confirmation question cannot be answered from
it; use
.BR \-\-yes .
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fIname\fR
This machine's name, shown to the key holder.
.TP
\fB\-\-machine\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This machine's persistent transport identity.
Default
.BR /etc/synctang/machine.pem .
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIduration\fR
How long to accept a pairing for.
Default 5 minutes.
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Enrol whoever answers the code, without asking for confirmation.
.TP
\fB\-\-direct\-addr\fR \fIhost:port\fR
Listen on this address directly, bypassing relay and discovery.
For testing only.
.TP
\fB\-\-network\-conf\fR \fIfile\fR
The file naming the relay pool and discovery servers to use instead of the
public ones.
Default
.BR /etc/synctang/network.conf .
The kernel arguments
.B rd.synctang.relay=
and
.B rd.synctang.discovery=
override it.
If the discovery server is set, it is put in the pairing link, so that the phone
looks the machine up there.
.TP
\fB\-\-qr\fR \fImode\fR
How to draw the QR code:
.BR auto ,
.BR blocks ,
.BR plain ,
.BR kitty ,
or
.BR iterm2 .
.B auto
uses a real image on a terminal known to show one, the plain ASCII code when
.B NO_COLOR
is set, and half blocks everywhere else.
A terminal multiplexer swallows image sequences, so choose
.B kitty
or
.B iterm2
explicitly there.
.SS status
Prints, without changing anything, the device, this machine's transport
identity (or that it has not been created yet), the machine name, the number of
the
.B mr\-1
token, and for each enrolled key holder its transport identity, keyslot and a
short key identifier.
Run it before a revocation: naming the wrong transport identity revokes the
wrong key holder.
If one transport identity is enrolled more than once, it prints a line starting
.B WARNING
that names its keyslots and keys and the command that repairs it, because
.B agent
refuses to answer for such a key holder.
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fIpath\fR
The LUKS2 device or image to inspect.
Default: found from
.B /proc/partitions
and the systemd ask-password directory.
.TP
\fB\-\-machine\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This machine's persistent transport identity.
Default
.BR /etc/synctang/machine.pem .
.SS devices
Lists the LUKS devices on this machine, so that the device to give
.B \-\-device
does not have to be guessed.
It reads the headers of the block devices in
.BR /proc/partitions ,
which takes root, and prints one line for each LUKS volume: the device, its UUID, its
size, the name it is open under (or
.BR \- ),
and what synctang has on it:
.B no token
for a volume nothing has been enrolled on,
.B token, no key holders
for a token with nobody left in it,
.B 1 key holder
or
.B N key holders
for an enrolled volume, and
.B LUKS1, not supported
for a volume in the older format, which synctang cannot use.
It exits with status 1 and says so when there is no LUKS device, or when it is not root.
.B enrol
and
.B pair
name this command, and list the volumes they find, when
.B \-\-device
is left out.
.SS agent
Runs the systemd password agent: it waits for a key holder to connect and to
answer one recovery attempt at a time, checks that the recovered secret really
opens the volume, and only then answers systemd's password request.
The console prompt stays live, so the passphrase can still be typed.
Its log goes to the systemd journal, through journald's socket, under the tag
.BR synctang\-unlocker ,
so that after the boot it can be read with
.B journalctl \-b \-t synctang\-unlocker
(add
.B \-o verbose
for the fields, one per attribute, in upper case).
Warnings and worse also go to the console; so does everything if the journal cannot be had,
which is said once.
It runs until it receives SIGTERM, which the dracut module sends before the
switch to the real root.
It is normally started by the dracut module, not by hand.
.PP
It chooses the volume by looking for a block device with an
.B mr\-1
token when
.B \-\-device
is not given, and checks again on every attempt, because the volume may not
have appeared yet when the agent starts.
It also makes sure a resolver is configured before each attempt, since the hook
runs before the network is necessarily up.
A failed attempt is reported and retried.
.PP
If more than one enrolled entry carries the transport identity of the key holder
that connected, the agent refuses before sending it anything and logs an error
naming the keyslots, instead of choosing one: a key that is no longer in use
would fail every unlock without saying why.
Run
.B status
to see them and
.B enrol \-\-remove \-\-keyslot
to remove the stale one.
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fIpath\fR
The LUKS2 device to unlock.
Default: discovered from
.B /proc/partitions
and the ask-password directory.
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fIname\fR
This machine's name, shown to the key holder.
.TP
\fB\-\-machine\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This machine's persistent transport identity.
Default
.BR /etc/synctang/machine.pem .
.TP
\fB\-\-ask\-password\-dir\fR \fIdir\fR
The systemd ask-password directory.
Default
.BR /run/systemd/ask\-password .
.TP
\fB\-\-direct\-addr\fR \fIhost:port\fR
Listen on this address directly, bypassing relay and discovery.
For testing only.
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-level\fR \fIlevel\fR
One of
.BR trace ,
.BR debug ,
.BR info ,
.BR warn ,
or
.BR error .
Default
.BR info .
.TP
\fB\-\-journal\fR \fImode\fR
Whether to write the log to the systemd journal:
.B auto
(the default: when journald's socket exists),
.B on
or
.BR off .
.TP
\fB\-\-network\-conf\fR \fIfile\fR
The file naming the relay pool and discovery servers to use instead of the
public ones.
Default
.BR /etc/synctang/network.conf .
The kernel arguments
.B rd.synctang.relay=
and
.B rd.synctang.discovery=
override it.
.SS open
Waits for a key holder to approve, then opens the LUKS volume on
.I device
as
.BI /dev/mapper/ name
and exits.
It is for a volume that is not asking for a password at boot, such as an
external drive plugged in later, because the agent only runs in the initrd.
Nothing keeps running afterwards.
Mounting is a separate step, and
.B cryptsetup close
.I name
closes the volume.
.PP
It needs root, and the volume must already have a key holder enrolled (see
.BR enrol
and
.BR pair ).
Without
.I name
the volume is called
.BI synctang\- first-eight-characters-of-its-UUID .
A name is letters, digits,
.BR _ ,
.B .
and
.BR \- ,
starts with a letter or digit, and is at most 63 characters.
A failed attempt is reported and followed by another until one works or the
time is up.
Flags may be written before or after the positional arguments.
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fIname\fR
This machine's name, shown to the key holder.
Default: the name given at enrolment.
.TP
\fB\-\-machine\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This machine's persistent transport identity.
Default
.BR /etc/synctang/machine.pem .
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIduration\fR
Give up waiting for a key holder after this long.
Default 5 minutes.
.TP
\fB\-\-direct\-addr\fR \fIhost:port\fR
Listen on this address directly, bypassing relay and discovery.
For testing only.
.TP
\fB\-\-network\-conf\fR \fIfile\fR
The file naming the relay pool and discovery servers to use instead of the
public ones.
Default
.BR /etc/synctang/network.conf .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command did what it was asked: enrolled, revoked or rotated a key holder,
paired, opened the volume, or printed the status.
.B agent
exits 0 when it is stopped by SIGTERM.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed.
The reason is printed on standard error with the prefix
.BR synctang\-unlocker: .
This includes a wrong passphrase, nobody pairing or approving within the time
allowed, and a setting in
.B network.conf
or on the kernel command line that is wrong.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong: an unknown command or flag, a missing required
flag, or flags that exclude each other.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/synctang/machine.pem
This machine's persistent transport identity, a certificate and key.
Its Device ID is what key holders look for, so it must be the same file when
the volume is enrolled and in the initrd; the dracut module copies it there.
Created when first needed, with mode 0600.
.TP
.B /etc/synctang/network.conf
The relay pool and discovery servers to use instead of the public ones.
Optional.
See
.BR synctang\-network.conf (5).
The dracut module copies it into the initrd when it exists, so rebuild the
initrd after changing it.
.TP
.B /run/systemd/ask\-password
The directory where systemd leaves its password requests, which
.B agent
answers.
.TP
.B mr\-1
The LUKS2 token type written into the volume's header, one token per volume,
holding the machine's name and transport identity and one entry per enrolled key
holder, which contains the secret only in encrypted form.
.SH KERNEL ARGUMENTS
These are read by the agent from
.BR /proc/cmdline ,
so they can be changed at the boot prompt.
.TP
.B rd.synctang=0
Do not start the agent.
The escape hatch for when the network path is what is broken, and the console
prompt is wanted alone.
.TP
.B rd.synctang.relay=\fIURL\fR
Use this relay pool, or one relay as
.BI relay:// host : port /?id= relay-device-id ,
instead of the public pool.
.TP
.B rd.synctang.discovery=\fIURL\fR
Use this discovery server instead of the public one.
It replaces the discovery server and the announce endpoint of
.BR network.conf .
.TP
.B rd.synctang.log=\fILEVEL\fR
How much the agent logs:
.BR trace ,
.BR debug ,
.B info
(the default),
.B warn
or
.BR error .
Any other value is ignored, with a message at boot, and the default is used.
.PP
A kernel argument wins over the file.
The URL syntax, including how a self-signed discovery server is trusted, is in
.BR synctang\-network.conf (5).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B pair
chooses how to draw the QR code from the terminal it finds itself in, when
.B \-\-qr
is
.BR auto :
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, the plain ASCII code is drawn.
.TP
.BR TERM ", " TMUX ", " STY ", " KITTY_WINDOW_ID ", " TERM_PROGRAM ", " LC_TERMINAL
Used to recognise a Kitty, Ghostty, iTerm2 or WezTerm terminal, which gets a
real image, and a terminal multiplexer, which does not.
.SH EXAMPLES
Enrol a laptop key holder, taking the passphrase from the shell without writing
it to a file:
.PP
.nf
read \-rsp 'LUKS passphrase: ' PASS
synctang\-unlocker enrol \-\-device /dev/sdXN \-\-name my\-laptop \e
  \-\-pubkey <public key> \-\-recipient\-transport\-id <transport id> \e
  \-\-existing\-passphrase\-file <(printf %s "$PASS")
.fi
.PP
Pair a phone, then rebuild the initrd so that it carries the machine identity:
.PP
.nf
synctang\-unlocker pair \-\-device /dev/sdXN \-\-name my\-machine \e
  \-\-existing\-passphrase\-file <(printf %s "$PASS")
dracut \-\-force
.fi
.PP
Show who can unlock the volume:
.PP
.nf
synctang\-unlocker status \-\-device /dev/sdXN
.fi
.PP
Revoke a key holder, and rotate another one's key:
.PP
.nf
synctang\-unlocker enrol \-\-device /dev/sdXN \-\-remove <transport id> \e
  \-\-existing\-passphrase\-file <(printf %s "$PASS")
synctang\-unlocker enrol \-\-device /dev/sdXN \-\-replace <transport id> \e
  \-\-pubkey <new public key> \-\-existing\-passphrase\-file <(printf %s "$PASS")
.fi
.PP
Open an external drive after boot, and close it again:
.PP
.nf
sudo synctang\-unlocker open /dev/sdb1 backup
sudo cryptsetup close backup
.fi
.SH SECURITY
One approval from a key holder is enough to hand the volume secret to whatever
presents the machine's Device ID, because a key holder can only tell the machine
by that identity.
A copy of the disk that also has the machine's identity can therefore recover
the secret from a single approval, and revoking the key holder later does not
stop the copy's keyslot from opening with it.
Approve only when you are rebooting that machine and can see its console.
See
.I docs/threat-model.md
in the source distribution for what synctang does and does not protect against.
.PP
The passphrase is handed to
.BR cryptsetup (8)
over a pipe and is not written to disk by this program; do not put it in a
file.
The relay and discovery servers see two Device IDs and the volume of traffic
between them, not what it carries.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR synctang\-keyholder (1),
.BR synctang\-network.conf (5),
.BR cryptsetup (8),
.BR dracut (8)
